      ],
      "text": "Donepezil:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " likely exaggerates succinylcholine-type muscle relaxation during anesthesia; expect a synergistic effect with succinylcholine, similar neuromuscular blockers or cholinergic agonists (eg, bethanechol)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Monitor closely for active or occult GI bleeding, especially with ulcer risk (eg, ulcer history, concurrent NSAIDs)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Overdose:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " cholinergic crisis (severe N/V, salivation, sweating, bradycardia, hypotension, respiratory depression, collapse, convulsions); increasing muscle weakness may cause death if respiratory muscles are involved. Tertiary anticholinergics such as atropine may be used as an antidote."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
